  "market_context": "Downtrend on GBP/USD 1-minute chart; recent break of structure to the downside created a supply zone with liquidity above. Analyst identified an unmitigated FVG and order block on the left side as high-probability short entry area.",
  "key_observation": "Price swept liquidity above the supply zone (marked as '$ Liquidity $') and reversed at the order block, confirming a liquidity grab rather than a valid change of character.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identified a break of structure (BOS) to the downside on the 1-minute GBP/USD chart, establishing bearish market structure.",
    "Step 2: Noted a supply zone formed by the BOS, with liquidity gathered above it (labeled '$ Liquidity $'), indicating potential stop-loss cluster.",
    "Step 3: Located a clean fair value gap (FVG) and corresponding order block on the left side of the chart, marking a high-probability supply zone for short entries.",
    "Step 4: Interpreted the upward move into the liquidity zone as a liquidity grab (not a valid CHoCH), expecting continuation downward upon retest of the order block.",
    "Step 5: Placed a sell limit order just below the order block, stop-loss above the order block, and planned to break even at 2x risk by closing half position."
  ],
  "outcome": "Trade executed as described; price swept liquidity above supply zone then reversed at order block, triggering the short entry.",
  "lessons": "Liquidity sweeps above supply zones often precede reversals into order blocks; distinguishing fake CHoCH from true structure breaks is critical for high-probability SMC entries.",
